// 31a controls — the premises 31a's findings rest on, measured rather than
// inferred, because a contrast asserted from a neighbouring example is not a
// measurement of this one.
//
//   bsc --src-root wayfinder/prototypes/31d-middleware-measured \
//       wayfinder/prototypes/31d-middleware-measured/Controls Bare 500     -> :over
//   ... same invocation, PathOf on an Assigned record                     -> :home
module Controls

// CONTROL 1 — guards DO prove exhaustive on a bare parameter.
//
// This compiles. The same pair written over a field projection
// (`Quota(r) when r.Tag > 100` / `when r.Tag <= 100`) does NOT — it reports
// `Quota is not exhaustive`. So the finding is "guards on a projection do not
// discharge the residual", not "guards never do", and middleware is squarely in
// the failing case because a stage dispatches on a field of the conn.
public atom Bare(int n)
Bare(n) when n > 100  -> :over
Bare(n) when n <= 100 -> :under

// CONTROL 2 — there is no map type, and the assigns channel is a list.
//
// `Assigns: map<atom, term>` is refused:
//
//     error: no type named map takes a type argument
//       the prelude has `list<T>`, `option<T>` and `result<T, E>`;
//       your own take one with `type map<T> = ...`.
//
// So Plug's `conn.assigns` — an open key/value channel any stage writes into
// without coordinating with the others — has no direct spelling. The list of
// pairs below is the substitute and it DOES compile, which is what keeps this
// an ergonomic cost rather than a missing construct: the field is declared
// centrally once, and after that a stage prepends its own key without the
// record growing and without any other stage knowing.
//
// What it gives up: the values are `term`, so a reader narrows at each use
// (ticket 11's boundary rule), and lookup is a list walk rather than a map get.
record Assigned { Path: atom, Assigns: list<(atom, term)> }

public atom PathOf(Assigned c)
PathOf(c) -> c.Path
